/bin/print from the Micronix 1.6 distribution

There is no source for print, so this is the disassembly, and print.c is that
disassembly read back into C.

    disas -f print.ctl print.dist

print.dist is the binary off inode 26 of disks/dist/1012-8_dist_2.IMD, 21632
bytes, mode 0711, dated 7 August 1982 - the second disk, and the one in /bin.
It is a pr-like formatter and not the spooler (that was lpr).  It has no
header: see ../newuser/README.  Whitesmith's runtime, c.ent at 474e and c.ents
at 417a.

The text runs to 5580 and the program's own part of it is only 010f to 0ca4.
The formatted output engine is at 0dbf and the library follows - stdio,
malloc, perror, the system call stubs and the arithmetic helpers.  Its
strings: ": Unknown argument" at 01f3, "%s Page %d %s" at 0855, "pr" at 094b,
the usage line at 0964.

THE FUNCTIONS, in address order

    010f  main        getargs; then each file, or the standard input
    0207  getargs     the options, and the arithmetic for the page
    03cc  (perror and exit)
    03e6  printfile   a line at a time into pieces; the last page at the end
    0410  getline     read a line, fold it
    05d2  addcell     keep a piece; a full page is written
    0625  flush       the page
    082a  blanks      n newlines
    0863  header      printf("%s Page %d %s", name, page, date)
    0888  puts
    08a8  numeric     a string of digits, none included
    08f3  strsave     malloc and copy; "pr" and exit if there is none
    098f  usage
    09a5  putcell     a piece into a row, tabs to multiples of eight
    0adc  atoi        through btoi at 31dc, which is handed 512
    0b0c  newpage     two blanks, the page number up, the header, two blanks
    0b36  trailer     four newlines
    0b50  getword     blanks and controls, then the printable run
    0be8  swidth      the width of a string: tab to eight, backspace one less
    0c73  skipblank

WHAT IT DOES

The options are words that begin with a dash and may come anywhere among the
names: -N is N columns, -lN lines to a page and -wN the width, defaults 1, 66
and 79.  Anything else - "-x", "--" - is the word and ": Unknown argument" on
the standard error, and a number of nothing, "-" or "-l", is 0 and the usage
line.  The usage line goes to the STANDARD OUTPUT.

The page is five lines of heading, the body, four lines at the foot.  The body
is the page length less nine, and the number of pieces a page holds is the
body times the columns.  The width of a column is the width over the columns.

The heading is two blank lines, "name Page n date" and two more - and the line
of the date is ctime's, which ends in a newline, which is why the second pair
is two blanks and not one.  The date is read once when the command starts.
Each file is numbered from 1.

A line is read in pieces of up to 512 characters.  It is cut into words (the
blanks and controls and then the printable run, as getword has it) and the
words laid onto a piece as long as each is narrower than what is left - the
column less two for the first piece of a line, the column less the new word
for the others.  A word that does not fit ends the piece, loses its leading
blanks and begins the next one.  An empty line is a piece of its own.

When the page is full its pieces are written, a column at a time: row r of the
page has piece r of column 1, piece body + r of column 2, and so on, each put
at its column's offset in a row of width + 2 blanks, tabs expanded against the
start of the piece.  The row is trimmed back from the width's end to its last
non-blank and then a newline put after it.

It is in the trim that the original has a small error that is reproduced here:
the test that a row is entirely blank compares the POINTER, not what it points
at, to a blank, and is never true, so a row that is entirely blank is written
as one blank and a newline.  That is the last page's padding - a partial page
is written out to its full number of rows - and every empty row in a column
layout.

THINGS THAT DO NOT WORK IN THE ORIGINAL

-N, -lN and -wN, here.  The numbers come out of btoi, and atoi gives it a
length of 512, so btoi works to the string's address plus 512 and stops
there.  The command's arguments are at the very top of memory - in usersim the
last of them ends at 0fffe - so the end wraps to a small number, the test is that the pointer
is already past it, and the number is 0.  -N and -lN are then the usage
message and -wN is a width of 0, which prints one character to a line.  The
documentation's example, "print -4 -l188 -w132 file", does not run.  They work
in print.c.  To test them against the original the sim was given a copy with
that one constant, at 0aec, changed from 512 to 13, and the options placed
before a last argument at least that long.

A line of exactly 512 characters has no room for its end, and the NUL goes
into the first byte of the buffer the next word is cut into, which is
beside it; the scan goes on out of the line into that word, and cuts it
again.  The last word of such a line comes out twice.  The buffers are
beside each other in print.c for that reason, and the comment there says so.

A line of 512 blanks runs away.  The copy of the blanks overwrites the buffer
it is reading from, 512 characters behind, and never finds the end, and the
original does not come back.  A 512 character word with no blanks at all
overflows the piece buffer into the variables after it and does not come back
either.  print.c stops both: getword cuts a word short at 511 characters,
which is as much as the buffer holds, and the scan still goes to its end.

A page length under nine leaves a negative number of rows, the allocation for
them is refused, and the original stops with status 1 and no message.  So does
print.c.

HOW IT WAS CHECKED

In usersim, against the original with that one constant changed - so, with
the options working - on a hundred and thirty lines of text with tabs; three
hundred random lines of words, tabs, backspaces and long words, with LF and
with CR LF; no newline at the end; blank lines; an empty file; and the
standard input; with one and two and three and five and six columns, widths
from 1 to 132, page lengths from 9 to 30, and two and three files at once.
The error messages and the usage line match, and so does the status for each
case that ends in an error.  The date line is the only one that is edited
out, since the two runs are not in the same second.

The exit status of the original is whatever was in BC after the last file
for a good run, and 1 for the standard input; print.c exits 0 for both.
